And here is the code related to the assert statement:

```html
<code>
assert sum([account.money for account in frm.accounts]) >= amnt, "@@@ total balance of all 'frm' accounts is not less than 'amnt'"
</code>
```

This assert statement checks that the total balance of the 'frm' accounts is not less than 'amnt'. If the total balance is less than 'amnt', the program will raise an AssertionError with the message "@@@ total balance of all 'frm' accounts is not less than 'amnt'".
--------------------Here are the assert statements related to the code:

```py
# @@@ total balance of all "frm" accounts is not less than "amnt"
total = sum([account.money for account in frm.accounts])
assert total >= amnt, f"Total balance of all accounts is less than {amnt}"
```
The total balance of all accounts of the "frm" person is being calculated by summing the money from all the accounts. Then an assert statement is used to check if this total is greater than or equal to the amount being transfered ("amnt"). If it is, then the transfer can be made. If not, then an AssertionError is raised with an informative message.
